---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config.js';

const description =
  'Season 3 of AEO Decoded goes deeper: longer episodes that take one Answer Engine Optimization topic at a time and work it through on real sites, held to one standard — a principle or a tested result.';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Season 3: going deeper',
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: SITE.author },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.studioName, url: SITE.studioUrl },
  mainEntityOfPage: new URL('/the-reset/', SITE.url).href,
};
---

<Base title="Season 3: Going Deeper" description={description} schema={schema} currentPath="/the-reset/">
  <article class="prose">
    <header class="episode-head">
      <p class="field field--blue">Season 3</p>
      <h1>Season 3: going deeper</h1>
      <p class="citable">
        <mark>Season 3 of AEO Decoded takes one Answer Engine Optimization topic at a
        time and goes deep on it — worked through on real sites, and held to one
        standard: a principle or a tested result.</mark>
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>Why go deeper</h2>
    <p>
      Seasons 1 and 2 covered the fundamentals of Answer Engine Optimization across a
      lot of ground: schema, entity clarity, trust signals, measurement. Covering
      that much ground meant most topics got one episode. A lot of the most useful
      detail lives in the follow-up questions — how a principle holds up on a real
      site, where it breaks, and what it depends on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Season 3 slows down and answers those follow-up questions. Episodes run
      longer, and each one stays with a single topic until it's worked through.
      Episode 3.3, "The 15 Web Standards Every Website Should Meet — Whether You
      Sell Stuff or Not," is the shape of the season: one subject, taken all the
      way.
    </p>

    <h2>The standard</h2>
    <p>
      Every Season 3 episode is either a <strong>principle</strong> — something true
      about how machines read the web that doesn't expire with the next model
      release — or a <strong>tested result</strong> — something tried on a real site,
      measured, and shown. Tactics that can't be verified don't make the cut.
    </p>

    <h2>The test bench</h2>
    <p>
      The AEO Decoded site is part of the work. It was rebuilt from WordPress to a
      custom edge architecture so every principle from the show runs live on its
      pages: machine-readable structure, schema, and fast delivery. When an episode
      makes a claim, the AEO Decoded site is one of the places it gets tested.
      <a href={SITE.studioUrl} rel="noopener">Irishguy Design Studio</a> builds the
      same system for clients.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>
      New to Answer Engine Optimization? Start with Seasons 1 and 2 in the
      <a href="/episodes/">AEO Decoded episode archive</a>. Already know the
      fundamentals? Start with Season 3.
    </p>
    <p>— {SITE.author}</p>

    <section class="cta-strip">
      <p class="field">Follow along</p>
      <h2>Season 3 is live.</h2>
      <p>
        Follow AEO Decoded on your favorite platform to get each new Season 3
        episode the day it publishes.
      </p>
      <a class="button" href="/#listen">Choose your podcast platform</a>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
